This page compares ogygia, an islands library for SvelteKit, with other frameworks on the same pages. You can reproduce every measurement from the repository under
[`bench/`](https://github.com/ogygiajs/ogygia/tree/main/bench).

## The benchmark

The same blog post (the same prose, the same five interactive widgets and the same CSS) is built four
ways, and we measure how much client JavaScript each version ships as the post gets longer. Islands ship JS only
for the widgets, so their payload stays **flat**, while whole-page hydration ships JS for the entire
document, so its payload **grows with the content**.

The method is a port of the
[interactive-blogs-benchmark](https://github.com/khromov/interactive-blogs-benchmark) by Stanislav
Khromov, with full credit to [@khromov](https://github.com/khromov). We run it on the latest version of each tool,
with ogygia added.

<BenchCharts />

## Reading it

- **Islands stay flat.** ogygia, Astro and Mochi ship the same amount of JS whether the post is 9k or 71k
  words, while SvelteKit's whole-page hydration climbs with the prose.
- **ogygia compared with the other island frameworks.** All of them ship **Svelte 5** islands, so this compares
  architecture alone, with no framework handicap. Astro's island runtime is the smallest,
  followed by Mochi and then ogygia. ogygia carries a somewhat larger baseline runtime in exchange for a much larger feature
  set: server islands, held, live and frozen regions, and full SvelteKit integration (see the matrix
  below).
- **The runtime is feature-selected.** ogygia ships only the features your app uses, so a
  load-only page never pays for the router, lakes or persistence. The runtime size for each profile is tracked
  in
  [`apps/palaestra/e2e/bundle-size.snapshot.json`](https://github.com/ogygiajs/ogygia/tree/main/apps/palaestra/e2e/bundle-size.snapshot.json),
  so it can only shrink over time.

## What you can build

On payload alone, the island frameworks end up close to each other, so the bigger difference between them is what each lets you build:

| Capability                                                        |   SvelteKit   |            Astro            |      Mochi       | ogygia |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | :-----------: | :-------------------------: | :--------------: | :----: |
| SSR / prerender                                                   |      ✅       |             ✅              |        ✅        |   ✅   |
| Partial hydration (islands)                                       | ❌ whole-page |             ✅              |        ✅        |   ✅   |
| Client JS **flat** as content grows                               |      ❌       |             ✅              |        ✅        |   ✅   |
| Islands are Svelte 5                                              |      ✅       |     ✅ (any framework)      |        ✅        |   ✅   |
| Per-component wake: `load`·`idle`·`visible`·`interaction`·`media` |      ❌       |    ➖ (no `interaction`)    |        ?         |   ✅   |
| Server islands (defer render to an endpoint)                      |      ✅       |             ✅              |        ?         |   ✅   |
| **Held regions**: `region()` values you place like data           |      ❌       |             ❌              |        ❌        |   ✅   |
| **Frozen regions** (lakes): zero-JS interactive-context subtrees  |      ❌       |             ❌              |        ❌        |   ✅   |
| **Live / streaming** regions                                      |      ❌       |             ❌              |        ?         |   ✅   |
| Built on SvelteKit: its adapters, hooks, `$app`, remote functions |      ✅       |      ❌ own framework       | ❌ own framework |   ✅   |
| Opt-in SPA router                                                 |      ✅       | ➖ (MPA + view transitions) |        ?         |   ✅   |
| Markdown content collections built in                             | ➖ add mdsvex |             ✅              |        ✅        |   ✅   |
| Feature-selected runtime (ship only what the app uses)            |       —       |              —              |        ❌        |   ✅   |

`?` means not verified for the benchmarked version. The trade-off is that ogygia carries a somewhat larger baseline runtime (see the size figures above) in return for the bottom third of this matrix, the region primitives and the Kit-native integration that none of the other frameworks here offer.

## Reproduce it

```bash
# hermetic (Docker): builds every framework + runs Lighthouse
docker compose -f bench/docker-compose.yml up --build

# isolated runtime size per feature profile (rolldown, brotli)
pnpm run bundle-size
```

The numbers on this page are regenerated from `bench/results/latest.json`, the exact JSON the
benchmark emits.
